const macroCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Carbohydrates — 4 kcal/g', note: 'The preferred fuel: sugars and starches are broken to glucose, the brain\'s default substrate. Fibre is the carbohydrate we digest by not digesting it.' },
    { math: '2 · Protein — 4 kcal/g', note: 'Twenty amino acids, nine of them essential. Protein builds and repairs; the body has no storage tank, so supply must be daily.' },
    { math: '3 · Fat — 9 kcal/g', note: 'The dense fuel and the carrier for vitamins A, D, E and K. Essential fatty acids (linoleic, alpha-linolenic) must come from food.' },
    { math: '4 · Micronutrients — zero kcal, non-negotiable', note: 'Vitamins and minerals run the chemistry: B vitamins as coenzymes, iron in haemoglobin, calcium as scaffold and signal. Small doses, large consequences.' },
    { math: '5 · Water — the forgotten nutrient', note: 'The medium for everything above. Losses of just a few percent of body water measurably degrade performance and mood.' },
  ],
};

const balanceCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'intake', label: 'energy intake', min: 1200, max: 4000, step: 50, value: 2400, unit: 'kcal' },
    { name: 'bmr', label: 'basal metabolic rate', min: 1200, max: 2200, step: 25, value: 1650, unit: 'kcal' },
    { name: 'activity', label: 'activity multiplier', min: 1.2, max: 2.2, step: 0.05, value: 1.55, unit: '×' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'estimated expenditure', expr: 'bmr*activity', unit: 'kcal' },
    { label: 'daily balance', expr: 'intake - bmr*activity', unit: 'kcal' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/nutrition-dietetics/foundational/nutrients-and-how-they-work">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Nutrition is the study of what food does inside the body. Its vocabulary is the nutrients:
    carbohydrates, proteins and fats supply energy and building material; vitamins and minerals run
    the chemistry; water is the medium. Every dietary argument you will ever read is, underneath,
    a claim about these six classes and how they interact.
  </p>
  <p>
    The discipline's first sorting move is the energy question: <strong>macronutrients</strong>
    (carbohydrate, protein, fat) supply metabolisable energy — measured in kilocalories (kcal;
    the "calorie" of food labels) at 4, 4 and 9 kcal/g respectively (alcohol, though not a
    nutrient, adds 7) — while <strong>micronutrients</strong> (vitamins, minerals) supply
    essentially none but make energy use possible at all. The analogy that survives every
    physiology course: macronutrients are the fuel and the building materials; micronutrients
    are the spark plugs and gaskets; water is the cooling system and the oil. An engine can
    have a full tank and still run badly if the gaskets are gone — which is exactly how
    deficiency diseases work in a well-fed but unbalanced diet.
  </p>
  <p>
    The widget walks the classes; the sections below give each its mechanism, its numbers and
    its failure modes.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the nutrient classes and what each is for" widget="equationStep" config={macroCfg} />

  <h2>Macronutrients: fuel and fabric</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Carbohydrate</strong> is the preferred fuel: digestible starches and sugars are
    broken to glucose, the brain's near-exclusive substrate (~120 g of glucose a day, mostly
    for the brain), and the muscles' fast fuel. Surplus carbohydrate is stored as
    <strong>glycogen</strong> — about 400–500 g in liver and muscle, roughly a day's reserve —
    and beyond that, converted to fat. <strong>Fibre</strong> is the carbohydrate we digest by
    not digesting it: soluble fibre slows absorption and feeds gut bacteria (whose fermentation
    products, short-chain fatty acids, are themselves absorbed fuels and signals); insoluble
    fibre accelerates transit. The physiological recommendation (25–30+ g/day) is less about
    roughage than about feeding the microbiome and slowing the glucose wave.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Protein</strong> is the body's fabric: twenty amino acids, nine of them
    <em>essential</em> (must come from food — the body cannot synthesise their carbon
    skeletons). Protein builds and repairs; the body has no storage tank, so supply must be
    daily, and quality is about the essential profile (a "complete" protein supplies all
    nine; plant sources can be combined). Requirements scale with body mass and use:
    the reference intake is ~0.8 g/kg/day for a sedentary adult, and perhaps 1.2–2.0 g/kg for
    athletes and the recovering ill. Surplus protein is not stored as muscle — its carbon is
    burned or converted, its nitrogen excreted as urea by the kidney, which is why
    mega-dosing has no storage benefit and some metabolic cost.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fat</strong> is the dense fuel (9 kcal/g — why energy-dense foods are usually
    fatty) and the carrier for vitamins A, D, E and K; it is also the stuff of every
    membrane and the raw material of hormones. Two fatty acids are <strong>essential</strong>:
    linoleic (omega-6) and alpha-linolenic (omega-3) — the longer-chain omega-3s (EPA, DHA,
    from oily fish) are conditionally required and drive much of the dietary-fat debate.
    Stored fat (adipose) is the body's true energy tank: ~9,000 kcal per kilogram against
    glycogen's ~2,000 total — the evolutionary explanation for why humans can go weeks
    without food but only days without water. Digestion matters here too: fat is
    emulsified by bile and hydrolysed by lipase, and its absorption is slow enough to
    extend satiety — one of the mechanisms behind "fat keeps you full".
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "carbs make you fat; fat makes you fat" — as though one
  macronutrient is the culprit. Thermodynamically, chronic weight gain requires sustained
  energy surplus, whatever its source; macronutrients differ in <em>how easily</em> they
  drive surplus (energy density, satiety, palatability) and in health effects at equal
  energy — not in their power to violate energy balance. The diet wars are about adherence
  and cardiometabolic quality, not metabolism-breaking magic.</p>

  <h2>Vitamins and minerals: the machinery's chemistry</h2>
  <p>
    Vitamins are organic molecules needed in milligram or microgram amounts because the body
    cannot make them (or not enough). The functional grouping is fat-soluble
    (<strong>A, D, E, K</strong> — stored, hence toxicity possible at high doses, absorbed
    with dietary fat) versus water-soluble (<strong>C</strong> and the <strong>B family</strong>
    — not stored much, hence needed regularly). The mechanism story is the point: B vitamins
    are <em>coenzymes</em> — thiamine (B1) sits at the centre of glucose metabolism
    (deficiency: beriberi), niacin is part of NAD (deficiency: pellagra), folate and B12 carry
    one-carbon units for DNA synthesis (deficiency: megaloblastic anaemia — and folate
    insufficiency in early pregnancy causes neural-tube defects, which is why flour is
    fortified); vitamin C is a cofactor for collagen hydroxylation (deficiency: scurvy —
    gums bleeding, wounds failing, exactly the collagen story); vitamin D is really a
    <em>pre-hormone</em> made in skin under UV light, completed by liver and kidney, that
    controls calcium absorption.
  </p>
  <p>
    Minerals are inorganic elements, and their logic is elements-in-structure or
    elements-in-chemistry. <strong>Calcium</strong> (about 1 kg in the body) is scaffold and
    signal — bones, but also the ion that triggers every muscle contraction. <strong>Iron</strong>
    sits in haemoglobin's haem and in mitochondrial cytochromes; deficiency (the world's most
    common micronutrient deficiency — anaemia, fatigue) and overload (poisoned liver; also
    genetic haemochromatosis) are both diseases, because the body controls iron by
    <em>absorption</em> alone, having no excretion route worth the name. <strong>Iodine</strong>
    is built into thyroid hormones (deficiency: goitre; in pregnancy, the leading preventable
    cause of intellectual disability — which is why iodised salt is one of public health's
    great wins). <strong>Sodium and potassium</strong> belong to the next section. The
    discipline's key insight: micronutrients run in <em>window</em>s — both ends of every
    nutrient curve are disease.
  </p>

  <h2>Water and electrolytes: the medium and its current</h2>
  <p>
    Water is the medium for everything above — 50–65% of body mass, the solvent of blood and
    cytoplasm, the reactant of hydrolysis, the coolant of sweating. Losses of just a few
    percent of body water measurably degrade performance and mood, and losses beyond ~10% are
    life-threatening — which is why thirst is regulated (though it lags exercise) and why
    water, not food, sets the survival clock. <strong>Electrolytes</strong> — sodium,
    potassium, chloride, and the divalent workhorses — give that water its properties: they
    set <em>osmolarity</em> (water follows solutes, so the body's water compartments track
    salt), <em>membrane voltage</em> (nerve impulses and heartbeats are sodium and potassium
    currents), and <em>fluid volume</em> (blood pressure responds to sodium).
  </p>
  <p>
    The quantitative picture: the body holds roughly 42 L of water in a 70 kg adult — about
    28 L inside cells, 14 outside (blood plasma plus interstitial fluid) — and sodium is the
    electrolyte that guards the outside while potassium guards the inside; the gradients
    between them, maintained by pumps, <em>are</em> the electrochemistry of nerve and muscle.
    Worked example, the athlete: an hour of hard exercise in heat can shed ~1 L of sweat,
    carrying about 1 g of sodium with it. Replacing water but not sodium can, in extreme
    cases, <em>dilute</em> the remaining body sodium — exercise-associated hyponatraemia,
    the documented cause of marathon deaths from over-drinking — while replacing neither
    degrades performance within minutes. The everyday corollary: most dietary advice
    recommends keeping sodium moderate (high intake is associated with raised blood pressure
    in salt-sensitive people) while raising potassium (fruit, vegetables) — the two ions
    pull blood pressure in opposite directions, and modern diets are usually wrong on both.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "you need eight glasses a day regardless" and "sweating
  means you need sports drinks". Water needs vary widely (food itself supplies ~20% of
  typical fluid intake; thirst is a workable guide in healthy people), and electrolyte
  replacement matters in proportion to sweat volume and duration — an hour at the gym is
  water, not marketing; a six-hour summer shift is both.</p>

  <h2>Energy balance, made quantitative</h2>
  <p>
    The body obeys thermodynamics. Expenditure is mostly your basal metabolic rate — the cost of
    simply being alive (breathing, heartbeat, ion pumping, protein turnover) —
    multiplied by an activity factor. Balance is intake minus expenditure, and
    it integrates daily: a sustained surplus is stored, a sustained deficit is drawn from reserves.
    Drag the sliders to see how fragile the balance actually is.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="intake vs expenditure — drag the sliders" widget="sliderLab" config={balanceCfg} />

  <p>
    Read the lab like a dietitian reading a case. Expenditure = BMR × activity (1650 × 1.55 ≈
    2558 kcal in the default): a surplus of 200 kcal/day — a biscuit's worth — integrates to
    about 73,000 kcal a year, potentially ~9 kg of adipose tissue if nothing else adjusts.
    In reality the body <em>does</em> adjust: metabolism adapts partially to deficit, and
    spontaneous activity shifts — which is why the lab's arithmetic is a first approximation
    (the famous "3500 kcal per pound" rule overstates long-term change by ignoring it), not a
    law. The professional use of the equation is direction and magnitude, not prophecy:
    BMR itself scales with lean body mass (another reason protein adequacy and resistance
    activity matter), and the activity multiplier spans a genuine two-fold range between a
    sedentary and a heavily active week.
  </p>

  <h2>From nutrients to diet: the practice of the discipline</h2>
  <p>
    People eat foods, not nutrients, and foods deliver them in packages with fibre, matrices and
    interactions that change what the body absorbs. That gap between nutrient chemistry and dietary
    pattern is where most public confusion lives — and where the discipline of dietetics does its
    work: assessment first, then counselling grounded in evidence rather than fashion.
  </p>
  <p>
    The matrix effect is not a nicety — it is chemistry. Iron from meat (haem iron) is absorbed
    several times more readily than plant (non-haem) iron, and plant absorption is further
    boosted by vitamin C at the same meal and inhibited by tea's tannins — so the same iron
    intake can produce different iron status depending on the company it keeps. Fat-soluble
    vitamins ride with dietary fat (a fat-free salad is, chemically, a vitamin A and K
    loss). Dietary assessment therefore works at both levels: population guidance is set in
    nutrients (the <em>Dietary Reference Intakes</em> — EAR, RDA, AI, UL: the requirement
    meeting 50% of people, the intake meeting 97–98%, the adequate intake where an RDA can't
    be set, and the tolerable upper limit), but counselling is delivered in foods and
    patterns, because compliance lives there. The assessment loop — food record or recall,
    biochemical markers, anthropometry (weight, BMI, waist), clinical signs — is the
    dietitian's clinical method, and its first rule is the unit's summary: fix the
    <em>pattern</em> against the <em>reference</em>, then check the <em>individual</em>
    against the marker. Deficiency and excess look alike in diets and differ only in the
    data; the discipline exists to keep that distinction honest.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three deeper currents run under the fundamentals. First, <strong>bioavailability and
    regulation</strong>: the body controls many nutrients at the absorption or excretion step
    (iron's absorption block, calcium's vitamin-D gate, sodium's kidney dial), so "intake"
    and "status" are related through a regulator — the reason supplement doses and food
    doses are not interchangeable, and why serum levels, not labels, are the clinical
    endpoint. Second, <strong>the gut microbiome</strong>: fibre's fermentation products
    (short-chain fatty acids) nourish colon cells and modulate immunity and appetite
    signalling, making the microbiota a quasi-organ whose diet <em>is</em> our diet — the
    most active research frontier in nutrition science, and the mechanistic bridge between
    "eat your fibre" and measurable physiology. Third, <strong>guidance in an age of
    noise</strong>: single-nutrient headlines ("fat is bad" → "sugar is bad") keep
    oversimplifying the actual evidence structure — randomised trials on hard endpoints are
    rare for diet, cohorts show associations confounded by everything else, and effects are
    small and pattern-dependent. The professional stance is unheroic and stable:
    adequacy (DRI-style windows), variety, energy balance, pattern-level evidence — the
    same six nutrient classes this chapter began with, held together by arithmetic rather
    than enthusiasm.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ACEND accreditation standards; Institute of Medicine, <em>Dietary Reference Intakes</em>; Gibney et al., <em>Introduction to Human Nutrition</em>; WHO guidelines on sodium and potassium intake.</p>
</TopicPage>
